        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The project explores the effects of radiation quality on the dose-response relationships for induction of telomere aberrations and gene mutations. Ultimately, it should relate these two potential cancer development processes by determining whether telomere-double strand break fusions are a significant contributor to the overall mutagenic response of a cell to ionizing radiation. Also, it explores various aspects of radiation-induced non-targeted (bystander) effects; novel aspects of the work include determining whether different radiation qualities vary in their abilities to induce bystander effects, and whether telomere-associated aberrations are induced as non-targeted responses. 
<p>
Telomere dysfunction has become an intensive subject for study and telomere-driven aberrations are considered important contributors to genomic instability. Investigators are studying two important aspects of this relationship. First, there has not yet been a detailed study of how different radiation qualities induce aberrations involving telomeres. Second, it is not yet established whether telomere-double strand break fusions are a significant contributor to viable chromosome alterations that actually produce the mutational events capable of driving carcinogenesis. 
<p>
Therefore, the goals were (i) to understand how radiation quality affects the induction of telomere aberrations and gene mutations, (ii) to determine whether a link may exist between telomere-double strand break fusions and mutagenesis, and (iii) to explore whether these radiations induce non-targeted effects. The primary goal of the bystander experiments is simply to ask whether radiation of different qualities induces non-targeted mutagenesis and telomere aberrations. Basically, we expect in each case to see either a yes or no response, although it is possible that they might see different magnitudes of response. The secondary goal was to determine the relative contributions of direct and non-targeted effects on the overall response.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology is embedded in the results below.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This research project explored the effects of radiation quality on the dose-response relationships for telomere aberrations and gene mutations. It was designed to relate these two potential cancer development processes by determining whether telomere-double strand break fusions are a significant contributor to the overall mutagenic response of a cell to ionizing radiation. Finally, it explored various aspects of radiation-induced non-targeted (bystander) effects; novel aspects of the work included determining whether different radiation qualities vary in their abilities to induce bystander signals, and whether telomere-associated aberrations can be induced as a non-targeted response. 
<p>
Telomere dysfunction has become an intensive subject of study and telomere-driven aberrations are considered important contributors to genomic instability. Investigators studied two important aspects of this relationship. First, there had not yet been a detailed study of how different radiation qualities induce aberrations involving telomeres. Second, it had not yet been established whether telomere-double strand break fusions are a significant contributor to viable chromosome alterations that actually produce the mutational events capable of driving carcinogenesis. 
<p>
Therefore, the goals were to (i) better understand how radiation quality affects the induction of telomere aberrations and gene mutations, (ii) to determine whether a link may exist between telomere-double strand break fusions and mutagenesis, and (iii) to explore whether these radiations induce non-targeted effects. 
<p>
Aim 1 was to produce detailed dose-response curves for mutagenesis and telomere-aberrations including telomere-double strand break fusions. Different radiations of varying energy and LET were examined, at doses of 0-100 cGy. These studies were done in a human lymphoblastoid cell model, which allowed examination of mutagenesis at the heterozygous thymidine kinase (TK) locus, as well as telomere aberrations. The experiments were done under conditions where the telomeres were either stable (capped) or unstable (uncapped, accomplished by reducing the levels of the telomere protection protein DNA-PKcs). 
<p>
Aim 2 was to extend the study of radiation quality on mutagenesis and telomere aberrations to the realm of non-targeted effects. Several questions were addressed, Including: <br>
a. Is non-targeted mutagenesis, known to be induced by low LET radiation, also radiation-quality dependent? How do non-targeted responses affect the overall shape of the dose-response curves for mutagenesis, for different radiation qualities? <br>
b. Are telomere-aberrations, including fusion with double strand breaks (DSBs), induced by bystander signals? Is there dependence on radiation-quality? <br>
c. There is a question whether radiation-induced double strand breaks trigger the production of bystander signals. Does a single double strand break in a fraction of cells produce a measurable bystander signal? <br>
d. As an extension of (c), if a single double strand break can generate a bystander signal, and a dysfunctional telomere can act like a double strand break, does a dysfunctional telomere produce bystander signal? 
<p>
Aim 3 was to determine if telomeres join with double strand breaks to produce viable mutants. Therefore this aim was designed to link telomere-dysfunction with the production of the types of mutations that can drive carcinogenesis. These experiments made use of a lymphoblast cell derivative that has a yeast I-Sce1 restriction site engineered into an intron of the active TK allele. Therefore introduction of the I-Sce1 enzyme produces a double strand break in that one site in the genome and nowhere else (fortuitously the18 base pair I-Sce1 sites do not occur randomly in the human genome). As in aim 1, the experiments were done under conditions where the telomeres are stable or unstable. Mutants that have lost TK function were analyzed by fluorescence in situ hybridization to determine the fraction that has joined TK sequence with telomere sequence.
<p>
